              {
                "text": "**Inspecting the Grid's Structure**\n\nI've started visualizing this grid problem. Initially, my focus is on the 7x7 inner space defined by the green walls (3s). Currently, I'm examining the outer boundary of the 3s, which forms a 9x9 box from rows 2 to 10. Understanding this structure is key to mapping the red and yellow ring pattern.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Grid's Components**\n\nI'm now focusing on the coordinates within the inner 7x7 grid defined by the green walls. Specifically, I'm noting the position of the extra '3' at row 3, column 6. I've begun to examine the distances from this point, considering both Chebyshev (L_inf) and Manhattan (L1) metrics. It seems the output patterns in Train 1 might reflect concentric rings centered on that specific coordinate. The values at distance 1 are all '4'. I see ring-like concentric patterns in the output data, which can be useful in my understanding.\n\n\n**Confirming Distance's Pattern**\n\nI've made a crucial breakthrough! It appears concentric Chebyshev (L-infinity) distances define the output patterns. The value alternates: odd distances yield 4 (yellow), even distances yield 2 (red), and the center point remains its original value (green). This holds true across Train 2 and Train 3, centering on the green pixel. It seems to be working, now.\n\n\n",
                "type": "reasoning_text"
              }
